course: dictionary
lesson: 7984
title: Recount: tell it, or count it again
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Recount can be a verb or a noun, and the words around it show which meaning you need. To recount means to tell a story or describe events in order: «He recounted his adventure.» Here recount is an action word.
explain: Put the same word together differently and it can mean to count again. Check your sums, then recount the sweets. This is a different sense, so look at the sentence to be sure which one the writer means.
explain: In English lessons, a recount is also a type of writing that retells real events in time order. «On Tuesday, Class 4 went to the seaside.» Time words like first, next, after that and finally keep the events in order.
explain: In an election, a recount is a second count of the votes when the result is very close. If your class vote for captain ends 14 to 14, you ask for a recount so that everyone can trust the answer.
fact: Recount means to tell events in order, as in «He recounted his adventure.»
fact: Recount can also mean to count again, to check that your total is right.
fact: A recount text retells real events in time order, usually in the past tense.
fact: A recount written by someone who was there uses «I» and «we»; one about other people uses he, she, they or a name, like «Jo visited Gran».
fact: In an election, a recount is a second count of votes when the result is very close.
exercise: «He recounted his adventure.» What does recounted mean here? | Forgot the adventure | Drew a map of the adventure | Counted the adventure again | *Told the story of what happened
exercise: Sam writes, «I went to the park. First we played football. After that we ate lunch.» Which person is this recount written in? | It is not a recount at all | *First person, because it uses I and we | Third person, because it uses he or she | Second person, because it uses you
exercise: The vote ended 500 to 499, so the team asked for a recount. What is a recount here? | *A second count of the votes | A new election next week | A speech by the winner | A prize for the winner
test: Which words help a recount show the order of events? | quickly, loudly, softly | *first, next, after that | because, although, but | blue, green, red
test: In maths, what does it mean to recount the apples? | *Count them again to check | Sort them by colour | Eat them all | Put them in a bag
exam: Which sentence uses recount to mean count again? | Tell me your recount of the day | Our class wrote a recount of the trip | The recount in the election was very close | *Please recount the chairs before we start
